{{!-- RELATIONSHIP MAP — who at this table knows whom, and how.

      One window per map (there are several named maps in a world), driven by
      module/dialogs/RelationshipMapWindow.js. The board inside it pans and zooms as a whole; the
      bar above it does not ride the board, so the controls stay put while the web moves behind them.

      ONE root element, as AppV1 requires. --}}
<div class="stonetop-relmap labels-{{labelMode}}{{#unless canEdit}} is-readonly{{/unless}}">

  {{!-- ⚠ NO MAP NAME ON THIS BAR. It used to open with the entry's name, which is the same name the
        window's own title bar is already carrying an inch above it — the map said twice, one line
        apart, for the width of a title. What is gone with it is the spacer that pushed everything
        else to the right-hand edge: the tools now start where the reader's eye starts. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-bar">
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-tools">

      {{!-- WHICH QUESTION THIS MAP IS BEING ASKED, and it is the first control on the bar because
            on a crowded board it is the one that helps most.

            Four readings of the same data: the whole web the table has arranged; only the player
            characters and the lines between them; one person and everybody with a line straight to
            them; and the family tree. The last three narrow
            the picture rather than rearrange it, because a board with forty people and two hundred
            lines is not badly laid out — it is being asked to say too much at once, and no layout
            wins that back.

            A `<select>` AND NOT FOUR PRESSED BUTTONS. One-of-four is a radio group's question, and
            a group is one tab stop with arrow keys inside it and a spoken name for the whole
            control; four toggles are four unrelated "pressed / not pressed" announcements with no
            group and no "2 of 4". It also FITS, which four labelled buttons do not: measured, they
            are 350-450px of chrome against this one's ~150, on a bar that was already over the edge
            at 1366px wide before anything was added to it. (The bar wraps now as well — that went
            in alongside this, as the backstop for a large font scale — but wrapping is what keeps a
            row that does not fit reachable, not a reason to build one that does not.) And its own
            value is the label, so the control says which view is up with no second element to keep
            in step.

            OUTSIDE the editing gate, like the captions control below and for the same reason: not
            one of these writes anything at all. The board is exactly as everybody left it. --}}
      <label class="stonetop-relmap-viewpick" data-tooltip="{{viewHint}}">
        <span class="stonetop-relmap-viewpick-label">{{viewLabel}}</span>
        <select data-relmap-view aria-label="{{viewLabel}}">
          {{#each views}}
          <option value="{{id}}" {{#if chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{label}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </label>

      {{#if showFocusPick}}
      {{!-- WHOSE WEB IS BEING SHOWN, and it stands right beside the view it belongs to so that the
            bar reads as one sentence: "Showing | One person's web | of | Ordga". Not a board tool:
            it writes nothing, and a player who may only read this map is exactly who wants to point
            it at themselves.

            A SECOND `<select>` RATHER THAN THE BUTTON THIS WAS. It used to open a modal list, which
            is a window to open and dismiss between every person — and walking the party one player
            at a time is the whole of what this view is for. Its own value is the label, so the
            control says who is in the middle without a second element to keep in step, and a native
            list has type-ahead, which is the search the modal was carrying.

            ⚠ ITS ROWS ARE DROPPED IN WHOLE, as the board is, and are built in
            `_focusOptions` — not written out here as an `{{#each}}`. They go out of date whenever
            somebody at the far end of the table puts a person on the map, and the repaint that
            fixes that can only reach the DOM. One builder, two writers; see `_paintFocusPick`. --}}
      <label class="stonetop-relmap-viewpick stonetop-relmap-viewpick--person"
             data-tooltip="{{focusHint}}">
        <span class="stonetop-relmap-viewpick-label">{{focusOf}}</span>
        <select data-relmap-focus aria-label="{{focusAria}}">{{{focusPick}}}</select>
      </label>
      {{/if}}

      {{!-- ⚠ NO "BRING THE PARTY IN" AND NO "BRING THE VILLAGE IN" BUTTONS. Both boards still seat
            themselves when the map is opened; what stood here was a pair of buttons asking for the
            same pass out loud, and the map already answers "somebody is missing from this board"
            twice over -- drag them on, or press "Add someone". --}}

      {{!-- MATCH ANSWERS TO PEOPLE. It draws what the introductions recorded onto the party's own
            board: this is how a table whose answers were recorded before the introductions asked
            "who is this about?" tells the
            board where to point. GM only, and it says so by not being there: what it writes is a
            world setting, which core refuses a player outright. --}}
      {{#if showMatchIntros}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="matchintros"
              data-tooltip="{{matchIntrosHint}}" aria-label="{{matchIntrosHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-people-arrows"></i>
        <span>{{matchIntrosLabel}}</span>
      </button>
      {{/if}}

      {{#if showFindKin}}
      {{!-- Only on the tree, where it is the thing a reader wants the moment they find it thinner
            than the map they know. It reads the writing on every unmarked line and marks the ones
            that name a tie, and says out loud how many, because a guess applied silently to
            somebody else's map is not a guess anybody can check. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="findkin"
              data-tooltip="{{findKinHint}}" aria-label="{{findKinHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-wand-magic-sparkles"></i>
        <span>{{findKinLabel}}</span>
      </button>
      {{/if}}

    {{!-- THE BOARD'S OWN TOOLS, and they are off while a narrow view is showing rather than merely
          disabled. Every one of them acts on the arrangement that view is not displaying: laying
          the board out again, adding somebody it would not draw, rubbing out lines it does not
          show. A button whose effect is invisible from where the reader is standing is worse than
          one that is not there. --}}
    {{#if showBoardTools}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="add"
              data-tooltip="{{addHint}}" aria-label="{{addHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-user-plus"></i>
        <span>{{addLabel}}</span>
      </button>

      {{!-- Lays the whole board out again, in a shape it ASKS for: a ring seated by who knows
            whom, or knots of people pulled together by their links. It asks every time rather than
            repeating whatever it did last, because the two are for different boards and different
            evenings, and a button that quietly picked one would be a button whose behaviour
            depends on something the reader cannot see. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="tidy"
              data-tooltip="{{tidyHint}}" aria-label="{{tidyHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-circle-nodes"></i>
        <span>{{tidyLabel}}</span>
      </button>

      {{!-- THE WAY OUT OF A BUTTON THAT IS GONE. "Pull in ratings" used to stand here: it wrote a
            line into this shared board for every rating anybody in the world had stored, one each
            way round, and a table that filled its sheets in during the introductions ended up with
            the handful of ties they drew by hand buried under a hundred they did not. What carries
            that information now is the board called "The Party", which seats the party by itself
            and draws what they actually answered about each other.

            This rubs out what the old button left behind, and only that: every line it made carries
            a stamp made at the moment of writing, so nothing anybody drew by hand is touched. The
            people stay on the map — taking somebody off is its own act, with its own question.

            ⚠ RENDERED ALWAYS AND HIDDEN, never behind an `{{#if}}`, for the reason the panels over
            the board are: `_paintChrome` can only write onto markup a repaint left standing, and
            whether this board still has any such lines changes the moment somebody at the far end
            of the table presses this. Behind a condition, their window would go on offering a
            button whose work was already done until they happened to re-render. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="droppulled"
              data-tooltip="{{dropPulledHint}}" aria-label="{{dropPulledHint}}"
              {{#unless hasPulled}}hidden{{/unless}}>
        <i class="fas fa-eraser"></i>
        <span>{{dropPulledLabel}}</span>
      </button>
    {{/if}}

      {{!-- HOW MUCH OF THE BOARD'S PROSE IS SHOWING, and it is OUTSIDE the gate above because it
            is not an edit. A player who may only read a crowded map is exactly the person who
            needs to be able to turn its captions down; leaving this with the editing tools would
            hand the one control that rescues a dense board to the one person who does not need it.

            A CYCLE RATHER THAN A MENU: three settings, each a press away, and the button says
            which one is on. A dropdown for three values is a second window to open and dismiss for
            something the reader wants to flick through while looking at the board.

            The accessible name is written by the window on every press, so it carries the setting
            with it; the visible label is only the current one.

            ASKED AFFIRMATIVELY of the view (`captions`), not as "am I not the tree?". The tree is
            the one view that draws no captions — what a descent line says is said by where it goes
            — and the two narrow views DO carry them, so the negation would have taken this control
            away from exactly the boards it was built for. It is also load-bearing beyond the
            markup: `_paintLabelMode` writes onto this element, and where it is missing it returns
            without writing anything, silently and with no test to notice. --}}
      {{#if captions}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool stonetop-relmap-tool--labels"
              data-relmap-action="labels" data-relmap-labels="{{labelMode}}"
              data-tooltip="{{labelsHint}}" aria-label="{{labelsAria}}">
        <i class="fas fa-comment-dots"></i>
        <span class="stonetop-relmap-tool-text">{{labelsLabel}}</span>
      </button>
      {{/if}}

      {{!-- TAKING A CHANGE BACK, AND PUTTING IT FORWARD AGAIN.

            AT THE END OF THE ROW AND IN THE SAME PLACE ON EVERY VIEW. Every other tool here comes
            and goes with what is being shown — the board tools are absent on a view that seats its
            own portraits, "Find family ties" only exists on the tree — and a pair the reader
            reaches for by muscle memory must not move when they change what they are looking at.
            These are also the only two controls on the bar that are about what the READER did
            rather than about the board, which is why they sit apart from the rest, after a rule.

            OUTSIDE `showBoardTools` FOR THAT REASON, and behind `canEdit` alone. What they reverse
            is whatever this reader last did to this board, wherever they were standing when they
            did it, and each press says out loud what it took back — so unlike "Add someone", the
            effect is never invisible from where the reader is.

            RENDERED DISABLED AND SWITCHED ON BY `_paintHistory`, never behind an `{{#if}}`. What
            can be taken back changes on every edit, and buttons that appeared and vanished as the
            reader worked would shuffle the whole bar sideways under the pointer. Their accessible
            name is written there too, and carries WHAT a press would take back — a reader who
            cannot see the board is exactly the one who needs telling before they press.

            ⚠ THE HISTORY IS THIS READER'S OWN and is never written to the map: see the head of
            module/relmap/relmap-history.js for why an undo here is a reversing write rather than a
            saved copy of the board, and why the table does not share one stack. --}}
      {{#if canEdit}}
      <span class="stonetop-relmap-history">
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="undo" disabled
                data-tooltip="{{undoNothing}}" aria-label="{{undoNothing}}">
          <i class="fas fa-rotate-left"></i>
          <span>{{undoLabel}}</span>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="redo" disabled
                data-tooltip="{{redoNothing}}" aria-label="{{redoNothing}}">
          <i class="fas fa-rotate-right"></i>
          <span>{{redoLabel}}</span>
        </button>
      </span>
      {{/if}}
    </div>
  </div>

  {{!-- WHICH BOARD OF THIS MAP IS UP.

        One map is several named boards, and each is a JournalEntryPage of the same entry carrying
        its own people, its own lines and its own layout (module/relmap/relmap-doc.js). A page is
        not another arrangement of one web: adding somebody to "The Millers" does not put them on
        "Marshedge", and the same person may stand on both, in different places, with different
        lines drawn to them. That is what actually thins out a village of forty.

        ITS OWN ROW, not another control on the bar above. The bar was measured over the edge at
        1366px wide before any of this, and — more to the point — this is not one of the bar's
        questions. The bar holds what the board can be asked and what can be done to it; this says
        which board that is, which is the frame everything on the bar happens inside.

        A TAB STRIP AND NOT A DROPDOWN, which is the one place this window deliberately spends the
        height. Pages are a thing a table has to KNOW it can make, and a `<select>` showing one
        value tells nobody that a second is possible; a row of tabs with a "+" on the end is the
        whole feature, visible. It is also where a reader's eye already goes to ask "which one of
        these am I looking at".

        ⚠ THE TABS ARE DROPPED IN WHOLE, as the board and the person chooser are, and are built in
        `_pageTabs` — never written out here as an `{{#each}}`. Somebody at the far end of the table
        adds and renames these under an open window, and the repaint that keeps up with that can
        only reach the DOM. One builder, two writers; see `_paintPages`.

        The strip is ONE tab stop with the arrow keys inside it (a roving tabindex, written by the
        same builder), because a map with eight boards is otherwise eight stops between the bar and
        the board for every reader who moves by keyboard. --}}
  {{#if showPages}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-pages">
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-pages-strip" role="tablist"
         aria-label="{{pagesLabel}}">{{{pageTabs}}}</div>

    {{!-- WHAT CAN BE DONE TO THE BOARDS, and all three are edits, so the whole group is behind the
          gate. Outside the scrolling strip beside it: a map with a dozen pages scrolls its tabs
          sideways, and tools that scrolled away with them would be tools a reader has to go looking
          for. Rename and delete act on the board that is UP — they are the current tab's own
          controls, standing where the current tab can always be seen. --}}
    {{#if canEdit}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-pages-tools">
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-page-tool" data-relmap-action="pagenew"
              data-tooltip="{{pageNewHint}}" aria-label="{{pageNewHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
        <span>{{pageNewLabel}}</span>
      </button>

      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-page-tool" data-relmap-action="pagerename"
              data-tooltip="{{pageRenameHint}}" aria-label="{{pageRenameHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-pen"></i>
        <span>{{pageRenameLabel}}</span>
      </button>

      {{!-- ⚠ ALWAYS RENDERED AND HIDDEN, never behind an `{{#if}}`, for the reason the two panels
            over the board are: `_paintPages` can only write onto markup a repaint left standing,
            and whether the last board may be rubbed out changes whenever anybody at the table adds
            or removes one. Behind a condition this button would exist on precisely the maps that
            already had two pages when the window last rendered — so a reader who adds a second
            page would have no way to remove it again until they reopened the window. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-page-tool stonetop-relmap-page-tool--drop"
              data-relmap-action="pagedelete" data-tooltip="{{pageDeleteHint}}"
              aria-label="{{pageDeleteHint}}" {{#unless canDropPage}}hidden{{/unless}}>
        <i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
        <span>{{pageDeleteLabel}}</span>
      </button>
    </div>
    {{/if}}
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{!-- The window the board moves behind: it clips, it does not scroll. A scroll container can only
        reach what is inside its own content box, so it would pin the board to its edges and grow
        scrollbars over a diagram that is meant to be dragged around freely.

        It is also the PANEL the strip above names, which is why it carries the tab roles' other
        half: a `role="tab"` with nothing saying what it controls is a tab in name only. The label
        is rewritten by `_paintPages`, because which tab is selected can change without a render —
        somebody else deleting the board this reader was standing on moves them to another. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-view" {{#if showPages}}role="tabpanel"
       aria-labelledby="{{pagePanelId}}"{{/if}}>
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-board">{{{board}}}</div>

    {{!-- Nobody on the map yet. Inside the viewport rather than instead of it, so the board is
          still there to be dropped onto — which is the other way somebody arrives.

          ALWAYS RENDERED, hidden rather than absent, because `_toggleEmpty` can only show and hide
          what the markup already has. Behind an `{{#if}}` it existed on exactly the maps that
          opened empty, so removing the last person from a populated one left a blank board with no
          lead text and no way in — the moment the panel is most wanted. The button inside it is
          still gated on permission, and the window re-renders whole either way.

          ⚠ THE PANEL MUST KEEP EXACTLY ONE CALL TO ACTION. This panel takes no pointer events (the
          board underneath it has to stay droppable), so its button opts back in — and it is the
          only thing inside the viewport a reader can tab to on a brand new map. It used to be "Add
          everyone", which put the whole cast on in one press; that button is gone, because a board
          with everybody on it is the crowding this window's views exist to escape. What replaced
          it is "Add someone", which is what the hint beside it already told the reader to use. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-empty" {{#unless empty}}hidden{{/unless}}>
      <p class="stonetop-relmap-empty-lead">{{emptyLead}}</p>
      <p class="stonetop-relmap-empty-hint">{{emptyHint}}</p>
      {{!-- ALWAYS RENDERED AND HIDDEN, for the reason its neighbour in the no-kin panel is: the
            window can only WRITE onto markup a repaint left standing, and which button this is
            depends on the view and on whether the reader may edit — both of which can change
            without the render that would otherwise have to put it there. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-cta stonetop-relmap-empty-cast"
              data-relmap-action="{{emptyAction.action}}" {{#unless emptyAction}}hidden{{/unless}}>
        <i class="fas {{emptyAction.icon}}"></i>
        <span>{{emptyAction.label}}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    {{!-- THIS VIEW IS SHOWING NOBODY, though the map has people on it. Its own panel and not a
          second state of the one above: a map with nobody on it and a map whose people this
          particular view does not draw are different situations, wanting different words and a
          different button.

          THREE SITUATIONS THROUGH ONE PANEL, because they want the same thing from the markup and
          differ only in what they say: no family tie marked anywhere, nobody on this map is a
          player character, nothing is linked to the person in the middle. Each is the picture a
          reader sees when a view looks broken and is not, so each has to be said in words rather
          than left as a blank sheet — and each needs a way out, which is what the button is. The
          window writes the words on every repaint, because they name a person and a count and both
          can change without a render.

          ALWAYS RENDERED and hidden, for the reason the empty panel gives. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-nokin" {{#unless noKin}}hidden{{/unless}}>
      <p class="stonetop-relmap-empty-lead">{{bareLead}}</p>
      <p class="stonetop-relmap-empty-hint">{{bareHint}}</p>
      {{!-- Wearing the empty panel's button class as well, because it is the same button in the
            same place doing the same job: the panel takes no pointer events so the board under it
            stays droppable, and that class is what opts one control back in.

            ⚠ ALWAYS RENDERED AND HIDDEN, exactly like the panel around it, and for a reason one
            step further on. `_paintChrome` can only write onto markup that is already there, and
            it is the only thing that runs on a repaint — so behind an `{{#if bareAction}}` this
            button existed on precisely the views that were ALREADY showing nobody when the window
            last rendered. Tick "hide the lines the sheets drew" and watch the focus ring empty, or
            have somebody at the far end of the table remove the last player character, and the
            panel would appear carrying its sentence and nothing to press. Its click handler is
            bound once per render, so it cannot be created here later either.

            The action, the words and the icon are all written by the window, because which of them
            this is depends on why the view is empty. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-cta stonetop-relmap-empty-cast"
              data-relmap-action="{{bareAction.action}}" {{#unless bareAction}}hidden{{/unless}}>
        <i class="fas {{bareAction.icon}}"></i>
        <span>{{bareAction.label}}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    {{!-- Who this view is NOT showing, said quietly in the corner rather than left to be noticed.
          A reader who knows this map has thirty people on it and is looking at nine needs to be
          told that the other twenty-one are still there, or the view looks like it has lost them.

          OUTSIDE the board and inside the viewport, so it stays put while the picture is panned and
          zoomed underneath it. --}}
    <p class="stonetop-relmap-aside" {{#unless omittedSaid}}hidden{{/unless}}>{{omittedSaid}}</p>

    {{!-- THE LINE THE READER HAS HOLD OF, and the four things they change about it most.

          WHY IT IS NOT THE DIALOG. Clicking a line used to open a modal window asking six
          questions, and five of them are questions nobody has: what a line SAYS, what colour it is,
          which way it is read and whether it is broken are the four things a table changes while
          talking, and a window that has to be opened, aimed at and dismissed for each of them is
          four windows between one thought and the next. The dialog still exists and still asks all
          six -- the family tie and the notes behind the line are real questions, they are simply
          not the ones being asked forty times an evening -- and the last button here opens it.

          THE WRITING FIELD IS FIRST AND TAKES THE FOCUS, which is the whole of "click a line and
          start typing". Everything else on the bar is a press.

          ⚠ INSIDE THE VIEWPORT AND OUTSIDE THE BOARD, which is the same choice the aside above
          makes and matters more here. The board's markup is REPLACED on every live update
          (`_repaintBoard` writes its innerHTML), so a bar that rode on the board would be destroyed
          under the reader's hands the moment anybody else at the table moved a portrait -- taking a
          half-typed caption and the focus with it. Out here it is only re-placed, by `place()`.

          ⚠ AND IT IS `hidden` RATHER THAN ABSENT, for the reason every panel in this window is:
          `RelmapTieBar` can only write onto markup a render left standing. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar" role="group" aria-label="{{tie.label}}" hidden>
      {{!-- No `list`: the caption suggestions belong to the dialog, where there is room for a popup
            to open downwards over something that is not the board. --}}
      <input type="text" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-words" data-relmap-tie="words"
             maxlength="{{maxLength}}" placeholder="{{tie.placeholder}}"
             aria-label="{{labelField}}" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">

      {{!-- THE COLOUR, as the eight swatches themselves rather than a swatch that opens a chooser.
            Eight presses' worth of bar against one press to open a second popup over the board and
            a second to choose in it: the swatches are wider and are still fewer gestures, and they
            say what the eight colours ARE without anybody having to go and look. Each carries its
            dash pattern too, which is what the high-contrast skin paints instead of the colour. --}}
      {{!-- ⚠ ONE TAB STOP PER GROUP, NOT ONE PER PRESS. A radiogroup is a single stop with the
            arrow keys inside it, and left as fourteen ordinary buttons this bar would put fourteen
            stops between the board and whatever comes after -- on a strip a reader arrives at by
            clicking a line, which is the worst place in the window to lose somebody. Every press
            starts at `-1` and `markChosen` (utils/relmap-tie-bar.js) hands the stop to whichever
            one is set, every time the bar opens. --}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-group stonetop-relmap-tiebar-inks" role="radiogroup"
           aria-label="{{inkLabel}}">
        {{#each inks}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-ink stonetop-relmap-ink--{{key}}"
                data-relmap-tie="ink" data-relmap-tie-value="{{key}}"
                role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1"
                data-tooltip="{{name}}" aria-label="{{name}}">
          <span class="stonetop-relmap-ink-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        {{/each}}
      </div>

      {{!-- WHICH WAY IT IS READ. Four presses and not three, because "no arrow at either end" is
            an answer as much as the other three are, and is the one most lines want.

            ⚠ THE TWO ONE-WAY BUTTONS ARE NOT LEFT AND RIGHT. Which of the pair is drawn on the left
            of the board is a fact about where two portraits happen to sit, and it changes when
            either of them is dragged; a fixed left arrow would mean "towards Sonya" on one board and
            "towards Mike" on the same board a moment later. So the icons and the words are BOTH
            written on open, by `RelmapTieBar`, from where the two faces actually are. --}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-group stonetop-relmap-tiebar-dirs" role="radiogroup"
           aria-label="{{dirLabel}}">
        {{#each dirs}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-btn" data-relmap-tie="dir"
                data-relmap-tie-value="{{key}}" role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1"
                data-tooltip="{{name}}" aria-label="{{name}}">
          <i class="fas {{icon}}"></i>
        </button>
        {{/each}}
      </div>

      {{!-- WHETHER THE STROKE IS BROKEN. A mark the reader makes on purpose -- the rumour against
            the fact -- and not the same thing as the dash an ink wears under the high-contrast
            skin. See RELMAP_DASHES in relmap/relmap-store.js, which says what that costs. --}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-group stonetop-relmap-tiebar-dashes" role="radiogroup"
           aria-label="{{dashLabel}}">
        {{#each dashes}}
        {{!-- ⚠ A DRAWN RULE AND NOT AN ICON. This was Font Awesome's `fa-ellipsis` for dotted,
              which is three dots in a row -- sitting two buttons away from `fa-ellipsis-vertical`,
              which is the "everything else" button. Two ellipses side by side on a strip this small
              read as one control, and the busier of them at 13px reads as broken type rather than
              as a line (the same failure the pin glyphs hit at that size). What each of these
              buttons means is a LINE, so each one draws its own. --}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-btn stonetop-relmap-tiebar-dash"
                data-relmap-tie="dash" data-relmap-tie-value="{{key}}"
                role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1"
                data-tooltip="{{name}}" aria-label="{{name}}">
          <span class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-rule stonetop-relmap-tiebar-rule--{{key}}"
                aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        {{/each}}
      </div>

      {{!-- EVERYTHING ELSE THIS LINE HAS, which is the dialog: the family tie, the notes behind it,
            and rubbing it out. Rubbing out is deliberately NOT on this bar -- one press with no
            question would be the only destructive gesture in this window that never asks, and the
            bar is the surface a reader clicks around on while talking. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-btn stonetop-relmap-tiebar-more"
              data-relmap-tie="more" data-tooltip="{{tie.more}}" aria-label="{{tie.more}}">
        <i class="fas fa-ellipsis-vertical"></i>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {{!-- THE LINES AN OLD IMPORT LEFT ON THIS BOARD, put away or brought back.

        DOWN HERE AND NOT ON THE BAR ABOVE, which is the one place in this window a control is
        offered twice over. The bar is the map's tools: things that change the map, or which
        question you are asking of it. This changes neither. It is a filter on what is drawn, it
        belongs to one reader, and it is the sort of thing that is looked for once and then left
        alone — so it sits out of the way of the buttons that get pressed all evening, in the
        corner where a board's own settings live.

        IT IS STILL WORTH HAVING NOW THAT NOTHING CREATES SUCH LINES, and it is the half of this
        pair that a PLAYER can reach: rubbing them out is an edit, and a reader who may only look
        still has to be able to see past a hundred of them. Nothing here is destroyed either way.

        RENDERED ALWAYS AND HIDDEN, like the two panels above and for the same reason: `_paint`
        can only show what the markup already has, and somebody at the far end of the table rubbing
        the last of these out is the moment this reader stops having anything to hide.

        The tooltip is on the LABEL, so resting on either the box or its words explains why anybody
        would want this — which is not obvious from four words, and is the whole reason the option
        exists. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-foot" {{#unless hasPulled}}hidden{{/unless}}>
    <label class="stonetop-relmap-foot-check" data-tooltip="{{pulledHint}}">
      <input type="checkbox" class="stonetop-check" data-relmap-action="hidepulled"
             {{#if hidePulled}}checked{{/if}}>
      <span>{{pulledLabel}}</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  {{!-- Spoken to a screen reader when something moves, and deliberately OUTSIDE the board: the
        board's markup is replaced wholesale on every repaint, and a live region that goes with it
        is a live region nothing is ever announced from. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-live" aria-live="polite" role="status"></div>
</div>
